1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,556
Question eighteen.

2
00:00:01,556 --> 00:00:07,433
Step one: macro scan. It asks why detail stays clear on a bigger TV.

3
00:00:07,433 --> 00:00:11,064
One mark. Command words: “mainly because”.

4
00:00:11,064 --> 00:00:12,619
Step two: denoise.

5
00:00:12,619 --> 00:00:16,163
Useful, in yellow: “details still clear”.

6
00:00:16,163 --> 00:00:20,139
In cyan: “mainly because” and “larger screen”.

7
00:00:20,139 --> 00:00:24,893
Useless red herring: phone and TV models — kick it out.

8
00:00:24,893 --> 00:00:26,449
Step three: draft.

9
00:00:26,449 --> 00:00:29,042
Detail depends on pixel count.

10
00:00:29,042 --> 00:00:32,327
More pixels stay clear on big screens.

11
00:00:32,327 --> 00:00:35,092
That is higher pixel resolution.

12
00:00:35,092 --> 00:00:36,562
Step four: P O E.

13
00:00:36,562 --> 00:00:40,538
A, lower bit rate: usually loses detail — cut.

14
00:00:40,538 --> 00:00:44,687
B, fewer audio tracks: sound, not picture — cut.

15
00:00:44,687 --> 00:00:48,663
C, smaller file: no guarantee of detail — cut.

16
00:00:48,663 --> 00:00:57,738
Step five: sanity check and lock. Clear detail comes from pixel count — consistent with pixel resolution.

17
00:00:57,738 --> 00:01:00,072
So the correct answer is D.

